==German==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|/ˌhalvəˈhaːn/|lang=de}}

===Etymology===
From Ripuarian dialect. The origin is possibly an order like ''Kann ich ne Halve hann?'' ("Can I get half [a sandwich]?"), the last two words of which were then re-interpreted (either by foreigners or jokingly) as ''halve Hahn'' ("half a rooster, half a chicken"). 

===Noun===
'''Halve Hahn''' {{g|m}} {{a|uninflected}}

# {{context|regional|in the Rhineland|lang=de}} a [[cheese]] [[sandwich]], cut in half.

====Usage notes====
The expression is rare in actual usage. Even in thick Ripuarian dialect one generally uses the plain and simple word ''Kiesbrütche'' ("cheese sandwich"). However, the term ''Halve Hahn'' is occasionally used as a [[shibboleth]] among Rhineland people and can be found on the menus of traditional Colognian pubs (as a humorous knowledge test for foreigners).

